        {
            "content_html": "<img src=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/46635587?u=b07ca7dea8057b32559bd35a0303b0f08f865478&v=4\" width=\"64\" height=\"64\" alt=\"\"/><br/><h3 dir=\"auto\">Description</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Clarify how lexical declarations interact with <code class=\"notranslate\">typeof undefined</code> checks when not initialized.</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Motivation</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/undefined#typeof_operator_and_undefined\" rel=\"nofollow\">current docs on <code class=\"notranslate\">undefined</code></a> aren't clear about the fact that variables created with <code class=\"notranslate\">let</code> and <code class=\"notranslate\">const</code> will throw a <code class=\"notranslate\">ReferenceError</code> if they're compared using <code class=\"notranslate\">typeof undefined</code> before initialization.</p>",
